<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Battle of La Puerta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venezuelan-war-of-independence">Venezuelan War of Independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Battle of La Puerta was fought on 15 June 1814 near San Juan de los Morros, Venezuela, between the patriot armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-marino">Santiago Mariño</a> and the royalist llanero cavalry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-tomas-boves">José Tomás Boves</a>. Boves won a crushing victory, opening the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, whose population fled east with Bolívar. The defeat led to the fall of the Second Republic of Venezuela.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle was fought between Royalists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> and the Second Republic of Venezuela. The Republicans had won the Battle of Carabobo on 28 May 1814 and, grown overconfident after that victory, chose to confront the irregular Royalist army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-tomas-boves">José Tomás Boves</a>, which was made up mainly of llanero horsemen. The two armies met in the gorges of La Puerta, near the Sémen River and San Juan de los Morros, in what is now the state of Guárico.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Boves and his forces inflicted heavy casualties on Bolívar's army, and many of the Republican soldiers were killed. After the battle more than 60 officers were taken prisoner, to be executed by firing squad. Among them was Colonel Diego Talon, who was hanged in Cura on the day after the battle. The surviving Republicans were forced to retreat from La Puerta, and Bolívar made for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 18 June Bolívar reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> with 400 survivors, closely pursued by the Royalist vanguard of Captain Ramón González, some 1,500 cavalry. On 25 June José Félix Ribas, with 400 cavalry, managed to stop González's division and delay its advance on the capital. Boves meanwhile chose to besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> first with the bulk of his army, and its garrison held out for 21 days.</p><p>The resistance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> gave the Republicans time to regroup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, but Ribas could gather fewer than 2,000 soldiers, and the capital could not be defended. On 6 July Bolívar evacuated Caracas together with two thirds of its population, in the long march known as the Emigration to the East. The defeat at La Puerta was a turning point in the war and led to the fall of the Second Republic of Venezuela.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Battle of La Puerta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Boves defeated Bolívar at <b>La Puerta</b> in June 1814, and the population of Caracas fled east in the Emigration to the East.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-republic-of-venezuela">Second Republic of Venezuela</a></li></ul></section>
